// Two echoes at the feet of two cliffs, each cut short by its cliff; count three from the open edge. Spouts and acid on the approach.
name Cut Echo
rule B1357/S1357
steps 3
hint A stranger law: a cell lives when its neighbour count is odd. Each echo grew three generations from a trench floor and the cliff cut off whatever reached it. Two seeds lie on the way. The echo's top is the only way up each cliff.
tip Three generations reach three cells out: the echo's far column stands three from the stone. Count from the open side, on the floor. It is not in the middle of what you can see.
intro Deeper in, the law is stranger. What lived here echoed, and the echoes climbed the cliffs before the rock cut them short.
outro An echo is the shape of its source, repeated and thinned. You are learning the grammar.
say wake|Vents ahead. They breathe fire on a count. Learn the count.
say plant|The cliff ate half of it. Count from the half you can see.
say grow|Odd neighbours live. Even ones do not. Watch it thin.
say found|Cut short, and still enough. Up.
say die|I have the light. You have the legs. Try once more.
